## The short version

Go find out whether your candidate problems are **real** by talking to people who actually have them. Produce **notes from 1–3 real conversations** (at least one is required) **plus one honest, evidence-tagged persona.** Capture the *needs you hear* — not solutions.

This is the founder's most important habit: understanding a real user's need well enough to speak for it.

---

## Why this assignment exists

In Session 2 you generated a big pool of candidate problems. Most of them are guesses. The only way to know which are real — and what the need actually is — is to **talk to the people who have them.** Session 4 taught you how to do that without fooling yourself (the Mom Test rules, non-leading questions). This assignment is where you do it for real. What you learn here becomes the backbone of your **Product Definition Brief (CP2)** and, later, your proposal.

---

## What to produce

A single document (or PDF), roughly **1–2 pages**, containing:

1. **Notes from 1–3 real conversations** (at least **one** required).
2. **One honest persona** built from the <course-link type="page" id="pg-persona-template">persona template</course-link>.

### Part 1 — Conversation notes (for each person you talk to)

- **Who they are** (role/relationship — anonymized; "a roommate," "a club officer," no full names needed).
- **The problem in their words** — what they actually said, not your paraphrase of your idea.
- **How they handle it today** — their current workaround (this is gold: it proves the problem is real).
- **What it costs them** — time, money, effort, or frustration.
- **One thing that surprised you** — something you didn't expect.

Keep it about *their life and their real, past behavior* — "tell me about the last time…," not "would you use…?" (See <course-link type="page" id="pg-good-vs-leading-questions">good vs. leading questions</course-link>.)

### Part 2 — One honest persona

Fill in the <course-link type="page" id="pg-persona-template">persona template</course-link> for a need you couldn't reach a real user for directly. Every claim tagged **`[E]` evidence** (with a source) or **`[A]` assumption** — no invented facts. List your sources and the top assumptions you'd verify.

---

## Finding people to talk to

**There are no assigned users — you find your own.** Talk to people who *actually have* the problem: roommates, classmates, family, coworkers, club/ward members, people in the affected community, online communities. One good, real conversation beats five hallway "sure, I'd use that" answers.

---

## Consent & ethics (required)

You're talking to real people. Follow the course norms (from the Good-vs-Leading handout):

- **Ask permission and say why:** *"I'm a student learning about [problem] — can I ask you a few questions? About 10 minutes."*
- **Be honest** — you're learning, not selling.
- **No recording without explicit permission** — notes are fine.
- **Respect their time and their "no."**
- **Protect privacy** — anonymize; no names or identifying details without consent.
- **Check with the instructor first** for sensitive topics or populations (minors, health information, anything you're unsure about).

---

## How it's graded

Graded on **honest engagement and insight**, not polish:

- You **actually talked to a real person** who has the problem (required).
- You captured **needs, not solutions** — their words, their behavior, their workarounds.
- You used **non-leading** questions (evident in what you recorded).
- Your persona is **honestly documented** — evidence vs. assumption clearly tagged, with sources.
- Reach at least a *Developing* level on each to stay on a passing track.

Perfect prose isn't the point; **truthful discovery is.** It's fine — good, even — to report that a problem you thought was real turned out not to be.

